\section {CSG objects} 
Just as a polyhedron can be described as the intersection of half spaces, a CSG (Constructive Solid Geometry) model describes a polyhedron as Boolean operation on primitives which are the simplest solid objects used for the representation. The Boolean operations on primitives are intersection, union, difference and complement. The primitives are solids. Suppose the equation of a surface is $f (x,y,z)=0$, the primitive decided by this surface are either $f (x,y,z)\le0$ or $f (x,y,z)\ge0$. For a polyhedron we define primitives as half spaces. But in more complicated cases, we need complex primitives like a cylinder, a sphere and so on.
\subsection {Primitives:}
These primitives comes from semigeometric definition and all these primitives are subsets of $\mathbb{R}^3$. 
\begin{enumerate}
      \item Half Space: A half space $H$ is a subset of $\mathbb{R}^3$, $H$ is defined by an inequation. Suppose the equation of a plane in $\mathbb{R}^3$ is $f (x,y,z)=a*x+b*y+c*z-d=0$ ,which is a linear function of coordinates$ (x,y,z)$, then the upper half space defined by equation $f (x,y,z)=0$ are $f (x,y,z)=a*x+b*y+c*z-d\ge0$, likewise the lower half space defined by equation $f (x,y,z)=0$ are $f (x,y,z)=a*x+b*y+c*z-d\le0$.
      \item Sphere : A sphere $S$ should also be a subset of $\mathbb{R}^3$. The boundary of the sphere are defined by an equation $f (x,y,z)= (x-x_0)^2+ (y-y_0)^2+ (z-z_0)^2-r^2=0$. When $f (x,y,z)\ge0$, we define the outer space of the sphere including the boundary. Or when $f (x,y,z)\le0$, we define the inner halp space including the boundary. 
      \item Cylinder : A Cylinder $C$ are a subset of $\mathbb{R}^3$. The boundary of this cylinder are defined by an equation $f (x,y,z)=0$ 
      
\end{enumerate}
\section {Subdivision trees:}
      \begin{enumerate}
            \item Box: B is a box $\iff B \subset \mathbb{R}^3$, $B=\{ (x,y,z)|x_1\le x\le x_2, y_1\le y\le y_2, z_1\le z\le z_2\}$, which is a regularized subset of $\mathbb{R}^3$
            \item ? Box of a polyhedron $P$: 
                  \begin{enumerate} 
            \item Empty box:
            A box $B$ is an empty box $\iff B\cap P=\phi$.
            \item Full box: 
            A box B is a full box of Polyhedron $P$ $\iff B\cap P = B$
            \item Vertex box: 
            A box B is a vertex box $\iff \exists ! v \in V$, and $v \in B$, s.t. $\forall e \in E$, if $e \cap B \not= \phi$, $v$ bounds $e$, and $\forall f \in F$, if $f \cap B \not= \phi$, $v$ bounds $F$.    
            \item Edge Box: 
            A box B is an edge box $\iff V \cap B = \phi and \exists! e\in E$, e\cap B \not= \phi, \forall f \in F, if f \cap B \not= \phi$, e bounds f. 
            \item Face box:
            A box B is a face box $\iff \forall e \in E, e \cap B = \phi, and \exists ! f \in F$, and f \cap B \not= \phi$. 

s.t. $B\cap e\not= \phi$, $B\cap f_i\not=\phi, B\cap f_j\not=\phi, \forall e_k\in E, e_k\not= e, B\cap e_k=\phi, \forall f_l\in F, f_l\not= f_i, f_l\not= f_j, B\cap f_l=\phi$.    
            \item Face Box: A face $f$ bounds a polyhedron $P$. This box should contain exactly a face cutting though the box into two parts. The intersection of the face and the boundary of the box are either a triangle or a parallelogram. \\
            A box B is a Face Box $\iff \exists f \in F$,s.t.$B \cap f \not= \phi$,
            $\forall f_i\in F$, if $f_i \not= f, B \cap f_i = \phi$.


                  \end{enumerate}
            \item Adequate subdivision tree: In $\mathbb{R}^3$, a subdivision tree are a octree in which each node of the tree would be a finite Box. The root are a finite box $B_0$. We can construct the tree by beginning with the $B_0$, and subdividing the tree until all leaves are ? Boxes.
      \end{enumerate}
